<text>

The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
if (!bs->sc->sa.bno_cur && xfs_btree_is_bno(bs->cur->bc_ops))
	bs->cur = NULL;

if (!bs->sc->sa.rmap_cur && xfs_btree_is_rmap(bs->cur->bc_ops))
	bs->cur = NULL;
```
Reason:
In these lines, `bs->cur->bc_ops` is dereferenced after `bs->sc->sa.bno_cur` or `bs->sc->sa.rmap_cur` may have been freed or set to NULL. Since `bs->cur` is aliased to one of those cursors (e.g., for bnobt scrub, `bs->cur` equals `bs->sc->sa.bno_cur`), when the aliased cursor is freed, `bs->cur` becomes a dangling pointer. Accessing `bs->cur->bc_ops` on a freed or NULL cursor causes a use-after-free (UAF) or null pointer dereference.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
+	is_bnobt = xfs_btree_is_bno(bs->cur->bc_ops);
+	is_rmapbt = xfs_btree_is_rmap(bs->cur->bc_ops);
...
-	if (!bs->sc->sa.bno_cur && xfs_btree_is_bno(bs->cur->bc_ops))
+	if (!bs->sc->sa.bno_cur && is_bnobt)
		bs->cur = NULL;

-	if (!bs->sc->sa.rmap_cur && xfs_btree_is_rmap(bs->cur->bc_ops))
+	if (!bs->sc->sa.rmap_cur && is_rmapbt)
		bs->cur = NULL;
```
Reason:
By sampling `is_bnobt` and `is_rmapbt` from `bs->cur->bc_ops` **before** any potential freeing or nullification of the aliased cursors, the check no longer depends on `bs->cur` being valid after those operations. The boolean values are safe to use later, preventing access to freed memory. This restores the correct temporal ordering that was broken when `xfs_btnum_t` was removed.

